More array fast paths in QuickJS-ng, with the same results. PokeRogue's sprite pipeline builds two
128-value colour arrays per sprite with 64 `arr.splice(arr.length, 0, ...src)` calls and flattens
them with `flat()`, twice per Pokemon per frame:
- ArraySpeciesCreate: a plain array whose Array.prototype.constructor and Array[Symbol.species] are
  still the built-in ones gets `new Array(len)` without the two property reads and the getter call.
- splice appending to a plain fast array (start == length, nothing deleted): the push fast path.
- apply / spread calls with up to 16 arguments from a plain fast array: a stack buffer instead of a
  heap allocation.
- flat() / FlattenIntoArray: non-object elements of a plain fast array appended to a plain fast
  array directly.
Standalone qjs (desktop, 2026-10-17): splice(len, 0, ...4 values) 0.98 -> 0.71 us, splice with 4
arguments 0.52 -> 0.42 us, flat() of 128 numbers 5.8 -> 2.1 us. QuickJS-ng's tests/*.js give the
same results with and without this patch (143 files, the same 15 non-zero exits), and so do edge
cases (frozen/sealed/non-extensible arrays, read-only length, subclasses, overridden constructor or
species, holes, index setters on Array.prototype, mutation during apply).

